{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":12000,"raw":false,"start_index":0,"url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/location/asia-and-the-pacific/east-asia/china/report-china/"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":12000,"raw":false,"start_index":0,"url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/location/asia-and-the-pacific/east-asia/china/report-china/"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://www.amnesty.org/en/location/asia-and-the-pacific/east-asia/china/report-china/:\n[Back to China](https://www.amnesty.org/en/location/asia-and-the-pacific/east-asia/china/)\n\n## China 2025\n\nChinese authorities intensified control over information and public discourse, and continued to suppress dissent and peaceful assembly. Participants in religious activities were persecuted through surveillance, raids and security laws. Human rights defenders, writers, artists and other civil society actors continued to be detained and prosecuted under vague national security provisions; censorship and online surveillance of these groups persisted. Authorities suppressed activism aimed at achieving gender equality and imposed restrictions on freedom of expression by women and LGBTI people, particularly in digital spaces. China continued to use the death penalty. Renewable energy capacity increased significantly, but fossil fuel consumption also continued to rise. Ethnic groups including Uyghurs and Tibetans remained under strict political and cultural control. In the Hong Kong and Macao Special Administrative Regions, national legislation further eroded human rights.\n\n## Background\n\nNine military leaders were removed from their posts, nominally as part of President and Chinese Communist Party (CCP) Secretary Xi Jinping’s “anti-corruption” agenda. In September, Xi Jinping launched the Global Governance Initiative, the most recent in a suite of development and cooperation plans underpinning his claims to be a leader in “genuine multilateralism”. Chinese businesses, often with close ties to the government, were involved in sustaining conflict globally, including in Myanmar and Sudan, and in facilitating violations by other states, such as Pakistan.\n\nSeveral incidents indicated growing transnational repression by Chinese authorities, including surveillance, censorship, harassment, legal actions and exerting leverage over other states to forcibly return people to China. These incidents targeted human rights defenders, activists, artists, and the Uyghur and Tibetan diaspora.[1](#footnote-china-2025-en-1) There were also reports of researchers or advocates in other countries facing funding cuts linked to their work and spurious defamation charges from Chinese companies for publishing material critical of their business practices.\n\n## Freedom of expression and association\n\nThe authorities intensified control over information, public discourse and freedom of association through new regulations, censorship and intimidation.\n\nOn 5 February, the National Radio and Television Administration introduced a new licensing system for all online micro dramas (short-form scripted videos), requiring prior approval before publication. The measure extended to content on major social media platforms including WeChat, Douyin and RedNote. On 8 February, the Cyberspace Administration of China, together with 11 other agencies including the Ministry of Public Security and the Ministry of State Security, issued the Internet Military Information Dissemination Management Measures, prohibiting the online production or sharing of any undisclosed information about national defence or the armed forces.\n\nJournalists and media outlets continued to face censorship. In June, news outlet Caixin and weekly newspaper Southern Weekly published investigative reports about an alleged death in police custody; both reports were swiftly deleted from official websites and social media platforms.\n\nOn 22 September, the Cyberspace Administration of China launched a two-month nationwide campaign targeting content on social media and short-video platforms that “incited hostility” or “spread pessimism”. Platforms were warned to delete such material and any other material deemed to discredit public institutions. In another example of tightened control, after the death of actor Yu Menglong, his fans’ posts of expressions of grief and criticism of online censorship were quickly removed from social media.\n\nWorkers and their advocates remained unable to exercise freedom of association. On 10 February, the ILO’s 2025 annual report raised concerns about the continued prohibition of independent trade unions and other restrictions on freedom of association in China.\n\n## Freedom of peaceful assembly\n\nAuthorities continued to restrict peaceful assembly through use of force by police and online censorship.\n\nDuring April and May, workers in several provinces, including Sichuan, Shaanxi, Hunan and Hebei, staged protests demanding payment of overdue wages. Labour rights monitors documented removal of related online posts and harassment of organizers, reflecting the authorities’ intolerance of collective action.\n\nIn August, reportedly more than 1,000 people gathered outside government offices in Jiangyou, Sichuan province, after video footage of a school bullying incident went viral. Police used batons and electric prods to disperse protesters; verified videos showed officers dragging and beating participants. Online discussion of the incident was swiftly censored, and residents reported being warned not to speak to foreign media.\n\nThat same month, a projection protest in the city of Chongqing displayed anti-government slogans for nearly an hour before police intervened. Videos of the projection were quickly deleted from social media platforms.\n\n## Freedom of religion and belief\n\nAuthorities intensified control over religious activities through surveillance, raids and prosecutions under anti-“cult” and security provisions. On 5 March, the Ethnic and Religious Affairs Department forcibly took over a church in the city of Huainan, Anhui province. Pastor Zhao Hongliang was sentenced to one year and one month in prison. On 10 March, police in Anhui province detained two Christians on suspicion of “organizing and using a cult to undermine law enforcement”.\n\nThroughout the year, practitioners of Falun Gong, a spiritual practice banned in China, continued to face imprisonment, including an 80-year-old woman in Beijing sentenced to five years in March.\n\nIn October, authorities launched a nationwide operation against the unofficial Zion Church network, detaining nearly 30 pastors and members in at least seven cities, including Beijing and Shanghai. At least 18 individuals remained in criminal detention at the end of the year. The campaign reflected continued efforts to eliminate unregistered religious activity and enforce state control over belief and worship.\n\n## Human rights defenders\n\nAuthorities continued to target human rights defenders, lawyers and activists under vague national security and public order provisions. Such measures were routinely used to suppress legal advocacy and activism, with prolonged pretrial detention, closed proceedings and restricted access to legal counsel.[2](#footnote-china-2025-en-2) Authorities continued to use “residential surveillance at a designated location”, a form of secret detention amounting to enforced disappearance, particularly against human rights defenders, rights lawyers and individuals investigated for national security offences.\n\nHuman rights lawyers and their families faced ongoing attacks. Activist Xu Yan was released from prison in early January after completing her one-year-and-nine-month sentence for “inciting subversion”. The appeal by her husband, human rights lawyer Yu Wensheng, against his sentence for the same offence was rejected on 6 January.[3](#footnote-china-2025-en-3) Lawyer Lu Siwei was sentenced to 11 months in prison in April for “illegally crossing national borders”. After his release in August, he continued to face restrictions on overseas travel. On 30 July, after more than three years in pretrial detention, lawyer Xie Yang was tried behind closed doors on charges of “inciting subversion of state power”.\n\nLabour and social rights defenders remained under tight surveillance. On 18 March, labour rights advocate Wang Jianbing was released after completing a three-and-a-half-year sentence. However, he was subjected to “deprivation of political rights” – an ambiguous, so-called supplemental sentence that violated international standards – and remained under surveillance. In April, vaccine safety campaigner He Fangmei’s appeal against her criminal sentence was dismissed.\n\nWriters, journalists and artists continued to face criminal prosecution for exercising their right to freedom of expression and their human rights work. On 6 January, film director Chen Pinlin was convicted under the ill-defined crime of “picking quarrels and provoking trouble” and sentenced to three and a half years in prison. On 19 September, journalist Zhang Zhan was tried, convicted and sentenced to a second four-year prison term for her human rights work.[4](#footnote-china-2025-en-4) Prominent artist Gao Zhen, detained since August 2024 on charges of “slandering China’s heroes and martyrs”, remained in detention at year’s end, awaiting trial.\n\nStudents and younger people were also targeted. On 31 July, 22-year-old student Zhang Yadi (Tara) was taken into custody by state security officials in Yunnan province after returning from university studies in France. She was reportedly being held on suspicion of “inciting separatism” for her peaceful volunteer work overseas. On 28 December, Uyghur university student Kamile Wayit was released from prison after completing her three-year imprisonment for purportedly “promoting extremism” by sharing social media posts.\n\n## Women’s rights\n\nAuthorities continued to suppress feminist activism and online discussion of gender equality. In August and September, the accounts of a number of feminist and women’s rights activists were permanently removed from the social media platform Weibo for “inciting gender antagonism”. State-linked media reported that the closures targeted content discussing sexual harassment and discrimination.\n\nIn October, Beijing hosted the Global Leaders’ Meeting on Women to mark the 30th anniversary of the Fourth World Conference on Women in 1995, where officials promoted what they called “historic achievements” in gender equality and women’s participation. Independent feminist voices were excluded from the event.\n\n## LGBTI people’s rights\n\nIn January, cancellations of shows by renowned performer and transgender woman Jin Xing raised concerns around tighter government control over public performances by LGBTI performers. Police in several provinces detained or questioned female authors of “boys’ love” (*danmei*) fiction for publishing homoerotic stories online. Media outlets and monitors reported that at least 30 writers were fined or placed under administrative detention as part of a campaign in June.\n\nIn September, the mainland release of the film *Together* was halted by its distributors after social media users discovered that a same-sex wedding scene had been digitally altered using AI to appear heterosexual. The decision drew widespread criticism on social media before related posts were censored.\n\nThat same month, the Cyberspace Administration of China ordered the lifestyle and social media app RedNote to “rectify” alleged failings to manage posts about LGBTI topics and women choosing to remain single. The regulator said such content “distorted values” and undermined “online civility”.\n\nOnline platforms, including Douyin, Bilibili and RedNote, continued to remove LGBTI-related groups and discussions under broad prohibitions of being “vulgar” or having “unhealthy” content, reflecting ongoing suppression of queer expression in digital spaces. In November, authorities ordered the removal of two popular gay dating apps from online platforms.\n\n## Death penalty\n\nChina continued to impose death sentences and carry out executions for a wide range of offences, including several that did not meet the threshold of the “most serious crimes” under international human rights law and standards. Official data on death sentences and executions remained classified as a state secret, making it very difficult to assess the government’s claim that the death penalty w\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 12000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"agent_message","text":"Review round 1 research is complete for all eight chapters. I continued the same immutable ruler-period scope: Xi Jinping, China, 2023. No score, score range, anchor, ranking recommendation, or judge advice was assigned.\n\nThe environment again rejected the attempted filesystem write with:\n\n> `bwrap: loopback: Failed RTM_NEWADDR: Operation not permitted`\n\nTherefore I could not update the supplied `research-materials.md` or `research-ledger-manifest.json`. The complete append-only continuation handoff is included below for parent persistence.\n\n# New evidence register entries\n\n## E009 — Arms Control Association: nuclear modernization and uncertainty\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2023-06/features/unknowns-about-chinas-nuclear-modernization-program#main|2023 nuclear modernization, doctrine uncertainty, alerting and centralized control`\n- **Source:** Fiona S. Cunningham, “The Unknowns About China’s Nuclear Modernization Program,” *Arms Control Today*, June 2023.\n- **URL:** https://www.armscontrol.org/act/2023-06/features/unknowns-about-chinas-nuclear-modernization-program\n- **Locator:** Article opening; section “A Thin Margin for Error”; discussion of alerting and warhead handling.\n- **Claim:** China was rapidly modernizing its nuclear capabilities, including more survivable retaliatory forces, silo construction, and systems that could provide coercive leverage in a conventional conflict. The article states that China historically maintained strict civilian control and a retaliatory/no-first-use posture, but available evidence did not establish a clear decision to adopt launch-on-warning or nuclear first use. It also describes uncertainty over revised launch procedures and shortened warning times.\n- **Source profile:** `medium_high`; arms-control analysis; `final_evidence`.\n- **Limitations:** Specialist analytical source rather than a primary Chinese record; motive and intent remain uncertain.\n- **Temporal fit:** Published June 2023 and directly addresses the target-period posture.\n- **Attribution:** The article’s opening caption states that Xi and the Central Military Commission must approve placing nuclear weapons on alert or launching them. Modernization is therefore highly relevant to top-level authority, but specific intent remains unresolved.\n- **Role/lenses:** Mixed/negative risk context for `1B.1`, `1B.2`, `1B.3`, `1B.5`, `1B.7`, `1B.8`, `1B.10`.\n- **Contrary/mitigating evidence:** Continued official no-first-use reaffirmation; no clear evidence that China adopted first use; historical centralized civilian control reduces accidental or unauthorized-use risk.\n\n## E010 — CSIS ChinaPower nuclear-force profile\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://chinapower.csis.org/china-nuclear-weapons/#historic-posture|China nuclear strategy, no-first-use, modernization and Xi-era force changes`\n- **Source:** ChinaPower Project, Center for Strategic and International Studies, “How is China Modernizing its Nuclear Forces?”\n- **URL:** https://chinapower.csis.org/china-nuclear-weapons/\n- **Locator:** Sections “China’s Historic Nuclear Strategy and Posture” and “China’s Shifting Nuclear Posture.”\n- **Claim:** China’s historical posture emphasized assured retaliation, low alert, a minimum-level arsenal, and no first use. Under Xi, China pursued sweeping PLA modernization and expansion of its nuclear forces, raising questions about whether its posture is changing.\n- **Source profile:** `medium_high`; research-institute analysis; `final_evidence` for technical synthesis, not sole proof of official intent.\n- **Temporal fit:** The page uses 2023/2024 open-source material and links the 2023 Pentagon report. Later data are not silently treated as 2023 facts.\n- **Attribution:** Xi-era modernization and earlier PLA reforms are attributed at leadership/policy level; specific operational decisions remain uncertain.\n- **Role/lenses:** `1B.1`, `1B.2`, `1B.3`, `1B.7`, `1B.8`, `1B.10`.\n- **Contrary/mitigating evidence:** Official no-first-use remains; the page notes uncertainty and skepticism around when China might depart from it.\n\n## E011 — 2023 Pentagon China military-power report\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://media.defense.gov/2023/Oct/19/2003323409/-1/-1/1/2023-military-and-security-developments-involving-the-peoples-republic-of-china.pdf#2023-report-nuclear-use-statement|2023 DoD assessment of Chinese nuclear policy`\n- **Source:** U.S. Department of Defense, *Military and Security Developments Involving the People’s Republic of China 2023*, 19 October 2023.\n- **Status:** `discovery_only`.\n- **Reason:** Direct retrieval was blocked by the source’s robots policy. Search results exposed a snippet stating that Xi said nuclear weapons must not be used and nuclear wars should not be fought, but no precise page locator or underlying text was retrieved.\n- **Potential mappings:** `1B.2`, `1B.3`, `1B.8`, `1B.10`.\n\n## E012 — CFR Taiwan security background and recent developments\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/confrontation-over-taiwan#recent-developments|PRC coercive Taiwan posture, military exercises and diplomatic alternatives`\n- **Source:** Council on Foreign Relations, “Confrontation Over Taiwan,” Global Conflict Tracker.\n- **URL:** https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/confrontation-over-taiwan\n- **Locator:** “Background” and “Recent Developments” sections.\n- **Claim:** The PRC claims Taiwan as part of China, states a preference for peaceful reunification while reserving force, and since 2016 has cut official communication with Taiwan while using economic sanctions, diplomatic pressure, and heightened military activity. The page records intensified air and naval activity and describes risks of miscalculation and severe global economic disruption.\n- **Source profile:** `medium_high`; intergovernmental/think-tank analysis; `final_evidence` for chronology and policy context, not sole evidence of Xi’s personal intent.\n- **Temporal fit:** Mixed historical and later-updated page. Only passages describing the 2023-era pattern should be used for the target year; the page’s later update date is a limitation.\n- **Attribution:** PRC/PLA policy; Xi-specific attribution is indirect except where the page discusses the current leadership trajectory.\n- **Role/lenses:** `2B.1`, `2B.2`, `2B.3`, `2B.6`, `2B.7`, `2B.10`; contextual support for `1B.8`.\n- **Contrary/mitigating evidence:** The PRC officially states a preference for peaceful reunification. Taiwan’s political choices and U.S. policy are relevant external factors. The page does not establish that Xi personally ordered each 2023 drill.\n\n## E013 — 2023 USCC annual-report candidate\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2023-11/Chapter_1_Section_2--U.S.-China_Security_and_Foreign_Affairs.pdf#2023-china-security-section|2023 USCC assessment of Chinese security and foreign affairs`\n- **Source:** U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission, 2023 Annual Report, Chapter 1 Section 2.\n- **Status:** `discovery_only`.\n- **Reason:** Direct retrieval returned PDF structural data but no usable extracted page text or precise page locator. Search results referenced a July 2023 report that Xi instructed the military to deepen war planning, but the underlying claim was not accepted without readable source text.\n- **Potential mappings:** `2B.1–2B.3`, `2B.6`, `2B.8`, `2B.10`; `8B.3`, `8B.9`.\n\n## E014 — Asia Maior analysis of Xi’s 2023 agenda\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.asiamaior.org/the-journal/18-asia-maior-vol-xxxiv-2023/china-in-2023-stimulating-economic-recovery-along-with-patriotic-education.html#introduction|2023 Xi agenda: recovery, ideological rectification, cadre training and technological self-reliance`\n- **Source:** “China in 2023: Stimulating Economic Recovery along with Patriotic Education,” *Asia Maior*, volume XXXIV.\n- **URL:** https://www.asiamaior.org/the-journal/18-asia-maior-vol-xxxiv-2023/china-in-2023-stimulating-economic-recovery-along-with-patriotic-education.html\n- **Locator:** “Introduction” and opening “Party and Politics” sections.\n- **Claims:**\n  - 2023 saw intensified Party ideological rectification through political gatherings, institutional reforms, and new laws.\n  - Xi’s leadership sought to project economic resilience after the pandemic.\n  - “Chinese-style modernization,” ideological unity, patriotic education, economic recovery, technological self-reliance, and cadre training were central parts of the leadership agenda.\n  - Senior-level CCDI investigations reportedly increased by 40% compared with 2022.\n  - Qin Gang, Li Shangfu, and other senior officials were removed during 2023.\n- **Source profile:** `medium_high`; academic/area-studies analysis; `final_evidence` for the article’s documented synthesis.\n- **Limitations:** Specific numerical or event claims should be corroborated with the article’s underlying sources where possible.\n- **Temporal fit:** Directly focused on 2023.\n- **Attribution:** Strongly attributes the agenda to Xi and the CCP leadership. Implementation and outcome claims remain partly inferential.\n- **Role/lenses:** `5B.1`, `5B.6`, `5B.7`, `5B.9`, `5B.10`; `7B.5`, `7B.7`, `7B.9`, `7B.10`; `8B.1–8B.10`; `4B.4`, `4B.5`, `4B.10`.\n- **Contrary/mitigating evidence:** The article notes discrepancies between Chinese and non-Chinese economic sources, a flawed recovery, and the possibility that security and authority priorities displaced stronger measures to support domestic demand and confidence.\n\n## E015 — World Bank China Economic Update, December 2023\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/cf2c1298e77c50bf1f1e7954ff560bc6-0070012023/original/China-Economic-Update-Dec23-EN.pdf#2023-household-property-income-and-economic-outlook|China household/property sector and macroeconomic constraints`\n- **Source:** World Bank, *China Economic Update — December 2023*.\n- **URL:** https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/cf2c1298e77c50bf1f1e7954ff560bc6-0070012023/original/China-Economic-Update-Dec23-EN.pdf\n- **Status:** `context`, pending locator recovery.\n- **Locator limitation:** The PDF was retrieved only as raw PDF structure; no reliable page/table locator could be extracted in this run.\n- **Claim from search/document metadata:** Fewer than half of Chinese households have property income, and property income is a small share of household income compared with advanced economies. The report addresses property-sector weakness and macroeconomic rebalancing.\n- **Source profile:** `high`; World Bank/intergovernmental economic analysis.\n- **Potential mappings:** `5B.3`, `5B.4`, `5B.8`, `5B.9`, `5B.10`; `6B.10`; `8B.7`, `8B.10`.\n- **Reason not counted as fully defensible:** Precise page/table locator was not recovered.\n\n## E016 — UNDP China in Numbers 2023 candidate\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.undp.org/sites/g/files/zskgke326/files/2024-03/china_in_numbers_2023-final.pdf#2023-youth-unemployment-and-human-development|China 2023 human-development and youth-employment indicators`\n- **Source:** UNDP, *China in Numbers (2023)*.\n- **URL:** https://www.undp.org/sites/g/files/zskgke326/files/2024-03/china_in_numbers_2023-final.pdf\n- **Status:** `discovery_only`.\n- **Reason:** UNDP robots policy blocked retrieval. Search results reported youth unemployment reaching 21.3% by June 2023, but no precise page/table locator was available.\n- **Potential mappings:** `5B.8`, `5B.9`, `5B.10`; `6B.2`, `6B.3`, `6B.8`, `6B.10`.\n\n## E017 — Asia Maior economic-recovery and policy-tradeoff analysis\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://www.asiamaior.org/the-journal/18-asia-maior-vol-xxxiv-2023/china-in-2023-stimulating-economic-recovery-along-with-patriotic-education.html#economy-analysis|2023 economic recovery, source discrepancies and security-versus-demand tradeoff`\n- **Source:** Same *Asia Maior* article as E014, distinct section-level claim.\n- **Locator:** Introduction, paragraphs discussing post-COVID recovery and the second-quarter slowdown.\n- **Claim:** The article reports a strong official recovery narrative but notes a significant second-quarter slowdown. It argues that prioritizing Xi’s authority, security challenges, and ideological modernization narrative may have displaced stronger measures to stimulate domestic demand and consumer confidence.\n- **Source profile:** `medium_high`; academic/area-studies analysis; `final_evidence` for the author’s interpretation, not sole proof of causal displacement.\n- **Temporal fit:** 2023-focused.\n- **Attribution:** Xi/CCP agenda attribution is explicit; causal claims remain analytical and qualified.\n- **Role/lenses:** Contrary/mixed evidence for `5B.3`, `5B.7`, `5B.9`, `5B.10`; `8B.7`, `8B.8`, `8B.9`, `8B.10`.\n- **Contrary points:** Official statistics reported growth and industrial expansion; the article does not claim economic collapse.\n\n## E018 — Freedom House minority-control and coercive social-policy context\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://freedomhouse.org/country/china/freedom-world/2023#xinjiang-tibet-minority-control|minority control, coercive education and demographic policy`\n- **Source:** Freedom House, *Freedom in the World 2023: China*.\n- **URL:** https://freedomhouse.org/country/china/freedom-world/2023\n- **Locator:** Retrieved opening paragraphs on Xinjiang, Tibet, minority regions, detention, forced sterilization, labor transfer, and boarding schools.\n- **Claims:** Freedom House reports coercive detention and political reeducation, forced-sterilization allegations, forced-labor and relocation concerns, and Mandarin-only boarding-school policies affecting Uyghur, Tibetan, and other minority communities.\n- **Source profile:** `medium_high`; rights-monitoring NGO; `final_evidence`.\n- **Limitations:** Advocacy orientation, source-access limitations, and mixed temporal coverage.\n- **Temporal fit:** The report is from 2023, but much evidence concerns policies beginning before or spanning the target year. Use as ruler-period/context evidence rather than a strictly 2023 event source.\n- **Attribution:** Chinese authorities and national policy; direct Xi personal attribution is not established by this page.\n- **Role/lenses:** `3B.1`, `3B.5`, `3B.7`, `3B.10`; `4B.3`, `4B.7`, `4B.10`; `6B.3`, `6B.8`; `8B.3`, `8B.5`, `8B.6`, `8B.10`.\n- **Contrary/mitigating evidence:** The government describes some programs as voluntary or educational; Freedom House disputes that characterization and cites external evidence. The disagreement must remain explicit.\n\n## E019 — Freedom House term-limit and entrenchment candidate\n\n- **Canonical fact key:**  \n  `https://freedomhouse.org/country/china/freedom-world/2023#term-limits|presidential term-limit removal and political entrenchment`\n- **Source:** Freedom House, *Freedom in the World 2023: China*.\n- **Status:** `context`, pending precise locator.\n- **Claim:** Xi orchestrated constitutional changes abolishing presidential term limits, clearing the way for continued tenure.\n- **Source profile:** `medium_high`; rights-monitoring NGO; context pending direct section extraction.\n- **Temporal limitation:** The underlying constitutional change occurred in 2018, outside the immutable target year. It can inform the inherited political baseline and 2023 succession context, but must not be treated as a 2023 event.\n- **Potential mappings:** `4B.1`, `4B.2`, `4B.4`, `4B.8`, `4B.10`; `8B.1`, `8B.3`, `8B.9`, `8B.10`.\n\n# Chapter continuation notes\n\n## 1B — Nuclear/existential risk\n\nE009 and E010 provide two independent technical/research source families. They establish:\n\n- rapid nuclear modernization during the target period;\n- increased survivability and diversified force options;\n- historical centralized civilian control;\n- continued no-first-use doctrine;\n- uncertainty over launch-on-warning and nuclear first-use adoption;\n- possible future or current coercive leverage in conventional conflict.\n\nThey do **not** establish:\n\n- a personal 2023 Xi decision to adopt first use;\n- unsafe command-and-control;\n- proliferation assistance;\n- a specific 2023 arms-control failure;\n- direct crisis de-escalation or escalation by Xi;\n- definitive change from the inherited posture.\n\nE011 remains discovery-only because the 2023 Pentagon report could not be retrieved to a precise locator.\n\n## 2B — International peace/security\n\nE012 adds a second independent source family beyond HRW/NBS for the Taiwan conflict context. It documents:\n\n- the PRC’s stated preference for peaceful reunification while reserving force;\n- cessation of official Taiwan communication after 2016;\n- economic and diplomatic coercion;\n- heightened military activity;\n- risks of miscalculation and global disruption.\n\nThe chapter still lacks a fully retrieved, precise 2023 event source establishing:\n\n- Xi’s personal order for a specific drill;\n- whether peaceful alternatives were available in the particular event;\n- defensive necessity versus coercion;\n- civilian or proxy conduct;\n- accountability and post-event correction.\n\nE013 remains discovery-only because its PDF text and page locator were not recovered.\n\n## 3B — Domestic safety\n\nE018 adds a second rights-monitoring family and specific minority-related evidence concerning:\n\n- detention and political reeducation;\n- alleged forced sterilization;\n- forced labor and relocation;\n- coercive education;\n- ethnic and religious identity suppression.\n\nTemporal fit is mixed and direct Xi attribution remains limited. It strengthens evidence for state-linked physical-integrity and vulnerable-group concerns, but not for every 2023 incident or for direct personal orders.\n\nRemaining gaps:\n\n- ordinary public safety;\n- non-state violence;\n- national prevalence;\n- crisis response;\n- effective remedies;\n- direct Xi knowledge, tolerance, correction, or obstruction;\n- target-year trajectory.\n\nThe prior E001–E004 items should be deterministically split by individual claim in the formatter. Their bundled structure is a serialization issue, not a reason to discard the underlying claims.\n\n## 4B — Political freedom\n\nE018 supplies non-HRW corroboration for coercive control of minority communities and constrained civic, religious, and cultural space.\n\nE019 is retained only as historical/context material pending precise locator recovery. It may inform the inherited baseline and succession context, but the constitutional amendment itself is not a 2023 event.\n\nRemaining gaps:\n\n- electoral contestability;\n- institutional capture and formal/de facto independence;\n- minority political equality in 2023;\n- succession and term-limit conduct specifically in 2023;\n- independent election-related evidence beyond HRW and local indicators;\n- trajectory from the immediate inherited baseline.\n\n## 5B — Economic wellbeing\n\nE014 and E017 add academic analysis of:\n\n- Xi’s explicit/revealed development agenda;\n- ideological and security priorities;\n- technological self-reliance;\n- cadre training;\n- official recovery narratives;\n- second-quarter slowdown;\n- possible displacement of domestic-demand support by security and authority priorities;\n- competing Chinese and non-Chinese assessments.\n\nE015 is a high-confidence World Bank candidate, but its precise page/table locator was not recovered and it remains context pending recovery.\n\nE016 is discovery-only because UNDP retrieval was blocked.\n\nRemaining gaps:\n\n- distribution and median/household welfare;\n- competition and property rules;\n- appointments and favoritism;\n- implementation quality;\n- independently corroborated official statistics;\n- counterfactual attribution;\n- policy correction and trajectory.\n\n## 6B — Social wellbeing\n\nE006 remains formal macro/outcome context rather than social-service evidence.\n\nE018 adds minority-related education, coercive relocation/labor, dignity, and distribution concerns, but is not a comprehensive social-service record and is partly pre-2023.\n\nE016 is discovery-only.\n\nRemaining gaps:\n\n- health-system delivery;\n- education quality and access;\n- water and sanitation;\n- housing;\n- social protection;\n- vulnerable-group distribution;\n- dignity and equal access;\n- professional management;\n- evidence-based correction;\n- durable social institutions.\n\n## 7B — Personal integrity\n\nE014 adds governance-network and anti-corruption leads:\n\n- increased senior-level CCDI investigations;\n- removal of Qin Gang and Li Shangfu;\n- removal of additional senior military officials;\n- competing interpretations of whether anti-corruption efforts strengthened institutions or prioritized Xi’s security and control.\n\nThis does **not** establish Xi’s personal integrity, dishonesty, self-dealing, or personal benefit.\n\nRemaining gaps:\n\n- Xi’s assets and family/business interests;\n- appointments and nepotism;\n- personal statements and corrections;\n- conflicts of interest;\n- deliberate concealment or deception;\n- direct personal benefit;\n- independent scrutiny of Xi’s own conduct;\n- meaningful response to scrutiny.\n\n## 8B — Effectiveness/competence\n\nE014 and E017 provide the strongest continuation evidence for a target-period program portfolio:\n\n- ideological consolidation and patriotic education;\n- Chinese-style modernization;\n- economic recovery;\n- technological self-reliance;\n- cadre training;\n- security and regime-control objectives.\n\nE009 and E010 provide a parallel strategic-modernization program involving nuclear forces.\n\nE018 documents implementation of coercive minority-control mechanisms relevant to regime-control execution, without treating that implementation as morally positive.\n\nRemaining gaps:\n\n- personnel competence;\n- cross-ministry and regional coordination;\n- adaptation after policy failure;\n- independent outcomes;\n- counterfactual attribution;\n- durability;\n- breadth across the full portfolio;\n- policy correction.\n\n# Local-fact disposition audit\n\n- **Used as context:** LF001–LF004 for 2B military-resource exposure.\n- **Used as context:** LF005–LF011 for 3B country-level repression and safety priors.\n- **Used as context:** LF012–LF029 for 4B political-freedom and institutional priors.\n- **Used as context:** LF015 and LF030–LF032 for 5B economic baseline and scale.\n- **Used as context:** LF012, LF028, LF033–LF036 for 7B institutional corruption/accountability conditions.\n- **Used as context:** LF012, LF014, LF028 for 8B governance-capacity conditions.\n- **Not used as direct ruler evidence:** all local facts, because none directly measures Xi’s personal conduct.\n- **Empty local states:** all 1B and 6B lenses remain explicit `no_evidence_found`; no absent local row was interpreted as absence of real-world evidence.\n- **Error dispositions:** none supplied.\n\n# Candidate dispositions for manifest update\n\nThe following entries should be appended to the existing manifest:\n\n- `E009` — `final_evidence`\n- `E010` — `final_evidence`\n- `E011` — `discovery_only`, reason: underlying source blocked and no precise locator\n- `E012` — `final_evidence`\n- `E013` — `discovery_only`, reason: PDF text/page locator unavailable\n- `E014` — `final_evidence`\n- `E015` — `context`, reason: high-value source but precise PDF locator unavailable\n- `E016` — `discovery_only`, reason: UNDP retrieval blocked and no precise locator\n- `E017` — `final_evidence`\n- `E018` — `final_evidence`\n- `E019` — `context`, reason: historical claim outside target year and precise direct locator not recovered\n\nNo previous item was silently reclassified."}}
